WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.300
-Our first guest tonight
is an actress,

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.167
a Grammy Award-winning musician,

00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:06.667
and a member of
the Country Music Hall of Fame.

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:09.334
Her new album and book,
both titled

00:00:09.334 --> 00:00:11.300
"Not That Fancy,"
are available now.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.701
She's also a coach
on "The Voice,"

00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:14.601
which airs Mondays
and Tuesdays here on NBC.

00:00:14.601 --> 00:00:15.467
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:16.968
-I want some tots before we go.

00:00:16.968 --> 00:00:19.167
-Yeah, let's do it.
-I want some tots.

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:21.701
-I am retiring
Reba's Tot Cart.

00:00:21.701 --> 00:00:23.801
-Reba's Team is full, y'all.

00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:27.801
-This is my first season
on "The Voice."

00:00:27.801 --> 00:00:30.367
I've had a blast
filling my team

00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:33.534
and I can't believe
how quickly it passed by.

00:00:33.534 --> 00:00:35.367
And that's what happens
when you're having fun.

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:37.501
I'll just be over here
with my tater tots.

00:00:37.501 --> 00:00:40.634
-[ Laughs ]
-Y'all need me.

00:00:40.634 --> 00:00:41.868
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:41.868 --> 00:00:44.000
the one, the only
Reba McIntyre, everybody.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:54.467
♪♪

00:00:54.467 --> 00:01:03.667
♪♪

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:04.767
Welcome back, Reba.

00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:06.534
What an honor to see you again.

00:01:06.534 --> 00:01:09.400
-So long since I've seen you.
-Well, it was last night.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.133
-Oh, was it? That's right.
-It was last night.

00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:12.534
-Time flies when you're
having fun.

00:01:12.534 --> 00:01:14.000
-We did a wonderful event at
town hall

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:14.934
for your book last night

00:01:14.934 --> 00:01:16.467
right here in New York City.

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:19.367
And my goodness, your fans
were out in full force.

00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:21.200
The people that love you,
they love you very much.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.501
And I hope you felt
it last night

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:23.801
because that was
a special evening.

00:01:23.801 --> 00:01:25.033
-I thought it was a lot of fun.

00:01:25.033 --> 00:01:26.834
You did a great job.

00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:28.434
-Thank you.
I'm very glad I did it

00:01:28.434 --> 00:01:29.968
because now I'm going
to make you tell another story

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:31.767
you told last night because
it was so good.

00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:34.667
You know, this book
is a very wonderful collection

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.000
of stories and anecdotes,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.100
and you can tell how much
your family means to you,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.534
generations of the McIntyres.

00:01:40.534 --> 00:01:41.934
But you were talking
about your mama.

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:44.167
And I think it was a story about
your grandmother needed a drink.

00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:45.167
Is that right?
-No, Mama.

00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:46.300
-It was your mama. Okay.
-Yeah.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.968
We were at her house

00:01:47.968 --> 00:01:50.801
and all the family was there
and the grandkids and kids

00:01:50.801 --> 00:01:52.567
and everybody
was running around.

00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:55.367
And Mama's nerves had just
gotten about right to here.

00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:57.667
And I looked over
at her and I said, "Mama,

00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:59.033
you need a shot of who hit him?"

00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:00.701
[ Laughter ]

00:02:00.701 --> 00:02:03.133
It's either a shot of who hit
him or a shot of who done it.

00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:04.734
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.734 --> 00:02:06.968
-And she said, "Yeah,"
She told Lou Casey,

00:02:06.968 --> 00:02:09.067
her granddaughter,
she said, "Lou, come here."

00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:11.067
She said, "I want you
to go in my bedroom.

00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:12.267
Go in the bathroom,

00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:14.033
look in the bottom
of my dirty clothes hamper

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:15.701
and bring me a bottle
of Crown out here."

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:17.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.667
She said, "Got it."
And I said, "Mama,

00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:20.434
why do you put your Crown
in the bottom

00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:22.534
of your dirty clothes hamper?"
She said,

00:02:22.534 --> 00:02:24.667
"Do you think my grandsons
are going to go in there

00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:26.767
checking for Crown
in my dirty clothes?"

00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:29.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.601 --> 00:02:31.300
-I love it.
She's one step ahead of them.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.400
-Telling you.
-She stayed one step

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.033
ahead of them.
-Absolutely. Always.

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:38.367
-Well, we were talking about
"The Voice" last night as well.

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:41.234
I mean, obviously,
there's a great camaraderie

00:02:41.234 --> 00:02:42.601
between you
and the other judges.

00:02:42.601 --> 00:02:44.801
But is there one of them
you have your eye on

00:02:44.801 --> 00:02:46.968
as the toughest competition?
-John.

00:02:46.968 --> 00:02:48.834
-Yeah.
-John Legend. Yeah.

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:51.968
John's got a great knack
for picking great singers.

00:02:51.968 --> 00:02:54.868
And when we're
with our backs

00:02:54.868 --> 00:02:57.734
to the singers
that come on to audition,

00:02:57.734 --> 00:02:59.467
I'm always kind of
going thataway,

00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:02.234
looking at John, seeing if
John's going to hit his button.

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:05.667
Well, John's really sneaky.
He won't do anything.

00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:08.033
He'll just be [Humming]

00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:10.968
And go like that.
And we're all like, "Oh, man!"

00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:12.334
-Yeah.
-But it's fun.

00:03:12.334 --> 00:03:14.133
-You seem like
you're having a fun time.

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:15.367
-I really am.
-It's lovely.

00:03:15.367 --> 00:03:18.734
You also -- I hope you had fun
writing this book

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:21.300
because like I said, it is
a collection of a lot of things.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:25.667
It's recipes, it's self-help,
it's your own story.

00:03:25.667 --> 00:03:28.868
And while you're writing it
and also the album as well,

00:03:28.868 --> 00:03:32.100
this is a collection of songs
you've done before, some new,

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.634
but mostly just a reimagining
of some wonderful songs.

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:39.033
And both of them are a real gift
to your, you know, your fans

00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:41.200
and people who maybe are going
to be a fan after they read it.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.434
But of these recipes,
can you just tell us real quick,

00:03:44.434 --> 00:03:46.934
your tater tots recipe,
because that is one that

00:03:46.934 --> 00:03:48.701
I'm the most confident
anyone can do.

00:03:48.701 --> 00:03:50.667
-Thank you very much.
Yes. You get in your car.

00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:51.968
-Yep.
-And you put it in reverse.

00:03:51.968 --> 00:03:54.000
Get out of the garage
and drive down the road

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.868
and you find your nearest Sonic.
-Yep.

00:03:58.868 --> 00:04:00.567
-Pull in the entrance,
not the exit.

00:04:00.567 --> 00:04:02.000
-Yep.
-And you pull in one of

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.834
them little slots and you push
that red button and you say,

00:04:04.834 --> 00:04:06.300
"Can I have me some tater tots?"

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.200
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.167
And I hope they don't say
"We're out."

00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:11.667
-Yeah. And you hope
you don't say they're out.

00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:13.334
Thank you for putting put
in the go in the entrance,

00:04:13.334 --> 00:04:15.434
not the exit, because I think
that's the one part

00:04:15.434 --> 00:04:17.033
I always get wrong
about the recipe.

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:19.133
-Well, yeah.
Yeah, I've done that.

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:21.868
-You do have this wonderful
new restaurant

00:04:21.868 --> 00:04:24.367
called Reba's Place in Atoka.
Am I saying that?

00:04:24.367 --> 00:04:25.567
Atoka, Oklahoma?
-Yes.

00:04:25.567 --> 00:04:28.634
-And that's also a music venue.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.033
-Yeah, it's got
a little bitty stage.

00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:31.801
-A little bitty stage.
But you were talking about

00:04:31.801 --> 00:04:33.567
how you kind of go in there
every now and then,

00:04:33.567 --> 00:04:35.634
and I would imagine it's called
Reba's Place, you're Reba.

00:04:35.634 --> 00:04:37.934
You walk in,
people get excited right away.

00:04:37.934 --> 00:04:39.534
But you were saying sometimes

00:04:39.534 --> 00:04:41.300
they just don't even
notice it's you.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:44.334
-Yeah, we did a surprise
appearance,

00:04:44.334 --> 00:04:47.300
and Pake, my older brother,
was up playing,

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.801
and he kind of got down
and my band snuck up there,

00:04:50.801 --> 00:04:54.167
three or four of them,
and I came through the kitchen

00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:56.834
and hopped up on the bandstand
and started singing

00:04:56.834 --> 00:05:00.434
"Can't Even Get the Blues"
and nobody said anything.

00:05:00.434 --> 00:05:01.901
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:05.634
I mean, they didn't even look up
from their chicken fried steak.

00:05:05.634 --> 00:05:07.133
They were serious about it.

00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:08.734
And all of a sudden I went
[Clears throat]

00:05:08.734 --> 00:05:11.367
and they went, "What is that?"
And they go...

00:05:11.367 --> 00:05:13.100
-That's pretty exciting.
-Yeah, well.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.400
-I guess they just
couldn't believe Reba would

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.100
actually be at Reba's Place.
But there you go.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.367
-I guess they thought it was,

00:05:17.367 --> 00:05:19.100
you know, playing over
the speakers or something.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.934
-It does speak
to the quality of the food.

00:05:20.934 --> 00:05:23.133
They won't even look up
from their chicken fried steak.

00:05:23.133 --> 00:05:24.801
-I'm going to use that.
-There you go.

00:05:24.801 --> 00:05:26.901
-Thank you.
-You have some farmers

00:05:26.901 --> 00:05:28.868
that are showing their love
for you

00:05:28.868 --> 00:05:31.868
in the launch of this book
and this album.

00:05:31.868 --> 00:05:34.667
I mean, I don't even know
how you go about this,

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.501
but I will say
it's incredibly impressive.

00:05:36.501 --> 00:05:37.534
These are -- I guess they're

00:05:37.534 --> 00:05:40.234
just using some real good
lawnmowers.

00:05:40.234 --> 00:05:43.300
So there's Von Thun Farms.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.434
-I'm asked,
"How do they do that?"

00:05:46.434 --> 00:05:50.167
-Here's another one.
That's a very good Reba as well.

00:05:50.167 --> 00:05:52.701
I mean...
-How about that?

00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:55.200
-I mean, it's worth
getting a small plane

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.734
just to go up and fly
around this country

00:05:56.734 --> 00:05:59.167
to see where your face
is turning up, right?

00:05:59.167 --> 00:06:02.868
-Do they put a computer chip
in the tractor or something?

00:06:02.868 --> 00:06:05.234
How do they do that?
-I think they might.

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:06.734
I was trying to figure it out,
but you're right.

00:06:06.734 --> 00:06:09.033
There's probably computers.
-No idea. Yeah.

00:06:09.033 --> 00:06:10.434
-Every time you see something,
you're like,

00:06:10.434 --> 00:06:12.200
"How did they do that?"
The answer It's a computer.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:14.467
Yeah.
-It's not just they found a guy

00:06:14.467 --> 00:06:15.934
who really knows
what you look like

00:06:15.934 --> 00:06:18.634
and put him behind a tractor,
and you're like, "Do your best."

00:06:18.634 --> 00:06:20.968
-Yeah. "Go for it."

00:06:20.968 --> 00:06:24.000
-Do you have fun
rerecording old songs?

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.067
-I do. Dave Cobb --

00:06:26.067 --> 00:06:30.534
He and I worked on this album
to strip it down

00:06:30.534 --> 00:06:33.367
and get just to the meat
of the song

00:06:33.367 --> 00:06:35.100
and not a big production,

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.868
which I had done originally
for these songs.

00:06:37.868 --> 00:06:40.934
And so it just really
brought out the --

00:06:40.934 --> 00:06:42.934
the heart and soul of the song,
I think.

00:06:42.934 --> 00:06:44.200
Like, for instance,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.033
"Consider Me Gone"
is one of the songs on it.

00:06:46.033 --> 00:06:47.667
That's Rex's favorite song.

00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:51.167
And it just -- I like
the song more now

00:06:51.167 --> 00:06:53.133
when I get to sing it
because it's softer.

00:06:53.133 --> 00:06:54.834
-Well, that's really wonderful.

00:06:54.834 --> 00:06:56.767
You know, I got to see you
do a few songs last night.

00:06:56.767 --> 00:06:59.200
I can't wait to listen to
the whole album start to finish,

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:00.501
and it's such a joy
to have you here.

00:07:00.501 --> 00:07:01.467
Thanks so much
for stopping by.

00:07:01.467 --> 00:07:02.701
-My pleasure.

00:07:02.701 --> 00:07:04.567
-That's Reba McIntyre,
everybody.

00:07:04.567 --> 00:07:05.868
"Not That Fancy,"

00:07:05.868 --> 00:07:08.567
the book and the album
are on sale now,

00:07:08.567 --> 00:07:10.167
and "The Voice" airs Mondays
and Tuesdays

00:07:10.167 --> 00:07:12.968
right here on NBC. We'll be
right back with Werner Herzog.

